   380	## 8. Growth of the reduced basis: exact classification — COMPUTED+VERIFIED
   381	
   382	For a trim deterministic automaton $\mathcal A$ let $c(L)$ be the number of accepted words of
   383	length $L$. (i) If no SCC contains a cycle, the language is finite. (ii) If some SCC contains a
   384	vertex with two internal out-edges, there are two distinct cycles $C_1,C_2$ through it, and the
   385	$2^k$ concatenations of $C_1^{|C_2|}$ and $C_2^{|C_1|}$ give $2^k$ distinct accepted words of one
   386	length (determinism), so $\limsup_L c(L)^{1/L}>1$: exponential growth along an arithmetic
   387	progression of lengths (not necessarily at every length, e.g. $(aa|bb)^*$). (iii) Otherwise every
   388	cyclic SCC is a simple cycle; with $d\ge1$ the maximal number of cyclic SCCs on an accepting path,
   389	$c(L)=O(L^{d-1})$, and $c(L)\ge cL^{d-1}$ for $L$ in an arithmetic progression (independent
   390	repetition counts of $d$ cycles). We call (iii) "polynomial of degree $d-1$". Applying this to the
   391	exact pair automaton of Theorem B (`work/classify.py`, trimmed to co-accessible states) gives, for
   392	the positive part of the reduced GS basis of the group:
   393	
   394	| type | orders with polynomial growth (degree) | all other orders |
   395	|---|---|---|
   396	| $I_2(m)$ | both orders: finite (Theorem C) | — |
   397	| $A_3$ | middle node largest (2 orders): degree 1 | exponential |
   398	| $B_3$ | $s_1<s_3<s_2$: degree 1; $s_3<s_1<s_2$: degree 2 | exponential |
   399	| $H_3$ ($s_1\overset5—s_2—s_3$) | $s_1<s_3<s_2$: degree 3; $s_3<s_1<s_2$: degree 2 | exponential |
   400	| $D_4$ | centre largest (6 orders): degree 4 | exponential |
   401	| $A_4,B_4,F_4,H_4$ | none | exponential (all 24 orders) |
   402	
   403	For the reduced GS basis of the **monoid** $A^+(W)$ (option `--monoid`,
   404	`evidence/classify/monoid.jsonl`) the table is the same except for $I_2(m)$, $m\ge3$: there the
   405	monoid basis is infinite of polynomial degree 0 (Theorem D(a); Autord, arXiv:0712.0525), while the
   406	group basis is finite; for $m=2$ both are finite.
   407	
   408	By Lemma 3, $P(W)\supseteq\mathrm{MinRed}(A^+_J)$ for every proper parabolic $J$; the monoid
   409	classification for $J=A_4,B_4$ therefore shows: for $A_n$ ($n\ge5$), $B_n$ ($n\ge5$), $D_n$
   410	($n\ge5$, which contain $A_4$) and $E_6,E_7,E_8$ (which contain $A_4$), **every** order gives
   411	exponential growth (in the sense of (ii)) of the number of rules of length $L$. Consequently, for
   412	these types and for $A_4,B_4,F_4,H_4$, no GS basis for any deg-lex order of our form has a set of
   413	leading words that is a finite union of families $u_0x_1^{p_1}u_1\cdots x_r^{p_r}u_r$ (such a union
   414	has polynomially bounded counts, and every GS basis contains all minimal non-normal words as
   415	leading words, Lemma 1(c)). This is a computer-assisted statement: it depends on Lemma 4/Theorem B,
   416	on the validated tables (§5), and on `work/automaton.py`/`work/classify.py`, whose output was
   417	cross-checked against the independent enumeration and the growth series.
