    11	## Problem
    12	
    13	The entry asks seven questions (book p. 33, verbatim in Status (a)). This target attacks
    14	**question (3)**. Formal statement, every modelling choice fixed in advance:
    15	
    16	- **Types.** Finite Coxeter systems $(W,S)$ ($A_n,B_n,D_n,E_{6,7,8},F_4,H_3,H_4,I_2(m)$ and products).
    17	- **Alphabet (the book's Artin–Garside alphabet).** $X=\{\sigma_s:s\in S\}\cup\{\Delta,\Delta^{-1}\}$, $\Delta$ the
    18	  positive lift of the longest element $w_0$. The book defines it for type $A$
    19	  ($\Delta=\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_1\cdots\sigma_{n-1}\cdots\sigma_1$); for every finite type the same definition via
    20	  $w_0$ (Garside, Brieskorn–Saito) is used. $A(W)$ is presented as a semigroup on $X$ by the braid
    21	  relations, $\Delta=$ (a positive word for $\Delta$), $\Delta\Delta^{-1}=\Delta^{-1}\Delta=1$.
    22	- **Monomial order (our choice; the book fixes none).** Deg-lex induced by
    23	  $\Delta^{-1}<\Delta<s_1<\dots<s_n$ for a fixed linear order of $S$, stated with each result (Bokut's
    24	  order for type $A$, arXiv:0806.1125). Other monomial orders are not treated.
    25	- **What counts as an answer.** The set of all valid relations is trivially closed under
    26	  compositions, so the content is an *explicit or finitely described reduced* GS basis for the
    27	  fixed order: (i) a finite list; (ii) a finite list of parametrized families; (iii) a finite
    28	  automaton for the leading words with right sides from the Garside normal form.
    29	- **Other alphabets in the book.** Artin–Burau generators (question 2) are defined in the book only
    30	  for type $A$; outside type $A$ no canonical analogue is given, so (2) is undefined there until a
    31	  generator dictionary is chosen. Birman–Ko–Lee generators (question 4) are defined only for type
    32	  $A$; a generalization needs a dual Garside structure (Bessis). Not attacked.
    33	- **Not this problem.** Convergent rewriting on the alphabet of *all* simple elements is known for
    34	  all finite types (State of the art); that is a different alphabet.
