   105	## State of the art
   106	
   107	- **Type $A$, book alphabets:** Artin–Garside (Bokut, JSC 43 (2008) 397–405), Artin–Burau
   108	  (Bokut–Chainikov–Shum, Comm. Algebra 35 (2007)), BKL with $\delta^{\pm1}$ (Bokut, J. Algebra 321 (2009)),
   109	  Adyan–Thurston (Chen–Zhong, Algebra Colloq. 20 (2013)); positive braid monoid (Bokut–Fong–Ke–Shiao 2003).
   110	- **Rank 2:** positive dihedral Artin monoids, infinite deg-lex completion (Autord, arXiv:0712.0525,
   111	  Prop. 3.4); all two-generator Artin groups in HNN/free-by-cyclic generators with a tower order
   112	  (Bokut–Vesnin, JSC 41 (2006)); standard signed generators shortlex automatic (Holt–Rees,
   113	  arXiv:1003.6007).
   114	- **Type $B_3$ positive monoid, standard generators:** Iqbal–Munir–Ayub–Nizami, Rev. Un. Mat.
   115	  Argentina 67(2) (2024) 503–516.
   116	- **All finite types, larger alphabets:** finite complete rewriting on all simple elements
   117	  (Hermiller–Meier, JPAA 136 (1999)); coherent convergent presentation on $W\setminus\{1\}$
   118	  (Gaussent–Guiraud–Malbos, arXiv:1203.5358, Prop. 3.2.1); finite quadratic convergent presentations
   119	  on finite Garside families for every finitely generated Artin–Tits monoid (Dehornoy–Guiraud,
   120	  arXiv:1504.02717).
   121	- **Regular normal forms:** lex-minimal positive braid words are regular in type $A$
   122	  (Flores–González-Meneses, arXiv:1808.02755); finite-state geodesic cross-sections on standard atoms
   123	  for all finite-type Artin monoids (Fuchiwaki–Fujii–Saito–Tsuchioka, RIMS 1646, 2008), not
   124	  lex-minimal. No source found giving a regular lex-minimal language on $\{\sigma_i,\Delta^{\pm1}\}$ for all
   125	  finite types.
   126	- **Coxeter groups (question 1):** finite types $A,B,D$ (Bokut–Shiao 2001), $E_6,E_7$ (D. Lee 2008),
   127	  $H_{2,3,4}$ (Lee–Lee, arXiv:1809.08419), $G_2,F_4,E_{6,7,8}$ (Pang–Wang, arXiv:2002.07369 v2, 2026);
   128	  Bokut–Shiao conjecture refuted (Chen–Liu, arXiv:0910.0096).
   129	
   130	## Attack
   131	
   132	1. **Reduction to Garside theory** (proof.md §§2–4). Lemma 1 turns the GS problem into finding the
   133	   minimal non-normal words and gives a counting criterion. Lemma 2: deg-lex normal forms are
   134	   $\Delta^{(k)}\mathrm{lexmin}(p)$. Theorem A: reduced basis = 2 cancellations + $2|S|$ twists
   135	   $s\Delta^{\pm1}\to\Delta^{\pm1}\varphi(s)$ + positive minimal non-normal words.
   136	2. **Right-complement automaton** (§5). Lemma 4 characterizes lexmin, $\Delta$-free words by right
   137	   complements; Theorem B builds a finite automaton for the leading words from $W$, for every finite
   138	   type ($N\ge2$) and order (`work/automaton.py`). Floating-point root tables are validated by exact
   139	   integer checks (`work/validate_tables.py`).
   140	3. **Rank 2 / finiteness** (§6): finite 8-rule basis with a full critical-pair proof; no finite
   141	   basis in rank $\ge3$ (explicit infinite families).
   142	4. **Closed forms.** Leading words were enumerated independently from the Garside normal form
   143	   (`work/gs_oracle.py`) and grouped (`work/families.py`); varying the generator order showed that
   144	   the bipartite order with the middle node largest gives bounded families in rank 3. $B_3$: 12
   145	   families by hand, proved by braid-move identities and a growth-series certificate (Theorem E).
   146	   Then families were read off the automaton automatically (`work/extract_families.py`) for $D_4$,
   147	   $H_3$ (two orders), $A_3$, $B_3$, and certified by the same pipeline (`work/certify.py`, Theorem F).
   148	5. **Growth classification** (§8, `work/classify.py`): exact SCC analysis for all orders of
   149	   $A_3,B_3,H_3,A_4,B_4,D_4,F_4,H_4$, plus parabolic inheritance; with Theorems E–F this gives the
   150	   classification of bounded bases (Corollary in §7).
   151	6. **Independent checks.** Knuth–Bendix completion (`work/kb.py`, sees only the defining relations)
   152	   reproduces the bases up to the truncation length ($B_3$ two orders, $A_3$, $G_2$, $H_2$, $D_4$, $B_3$
   153	   monoid); automaton NF counts equal the growth series.
   154	7. **Published $B_3$ monoid basis** (§10): checked Iqbal et al. 2024; three relations misprinted.
   155	
   156	Failures and limits: no order gives bounded families for $A_4,B_4,F_4,H_4$, so closed forms for them
   157	must contain non-bounded parts (e.g. free sub-words as in Bokut's type $A$); none were written. $E_7,E_8$
   158	automata were not built (tables too large for the implementation); their growth follows from the $A_4$
   159	parabolic. A first family extraction for $D_4$ timed out (exponential split search in the fitter);
   160	fixed by a faster normal form and a bounded search. The first certificate version over-approximated
   161	right sides with consecutive powers (shared NFA loop state); found via a false "reducible right side"
   162	alarm for $D_4$, fixed with epsilon moves; the affected check could only err on the safe side, and
   163	all certificates were re-run.
