   234	it stated in the literature we searched (README); we claim no novelty.
   235	
   236	**Theorem D (finiteness).** For finite $W$ (any order of $S$, deg-lex with $\Delta^{-1}<\Delta<S$),
   237	$A(W)$ has a finite GS basis on the Artin–Garside alphabet iff $\operatorname{rank}W\le2$.
   238	
   239	*Proof.* "If": Theorem C. "Only if": by Lemma 1(c) it suffices that MinRed is infinite.
   240	
   241	(a) Some $s<t$ has $m=m_{st}\ge3$. Put $u_i=t\,v_i$ with $v_i=s^i\langle st\rangle_m$, $i\ge1$. We
   242	show $u_i\in\mathrm{MinRed}(A^+_{\{s,t\}})$; since $|S|\ge3$, Lemma 3 then gives $u_i\in P(W)$.
   243	An alternating word of length $<m$ contains no side of a braid relation, so it is the only word
   244	of its element; the same holds for any word without alternating factor of length $m$.
   245	 * $v_i$ is lexmin: the only letter with a smaller letter is $t$; after an occurrence of $t$ in
   246	   $v_i$ the suffix is an alternating word of length $\le m-2$, the only word of its element, so
   247	   it is not divisible by $\Pi_{m-1}(s,t)$ (length $m-1$); Lemma 4(L) holds everywhere.
   248	 * $t\,v_i'$ is lexmin, $v_i'=s^i\langle st\rangle_{m-1}$: $v_i'$ has no alternating factor of length $m$,
   249	   so it is the only word of its element; it begins with $ss$ while $\Pi_{m-1}(s,t)$ begins with
   250	   $st$ ($m-1\ge2$), so $\Pi_{m-1}(s,t)\not\preceq[v_i']$; the other occurrences of $t$ are handled as for $v_i$.
   251	 * $u_i$ is not lexmin: $[v_i]=[s^i]\,\Delta_{st}=\Delta_{st}\,\varphi_{st}([s^i])$, so
   252	   $\Pi_{m-1}(s,t)\preceq\Delta_{st}\preceq[v_i]$, i.e. $s\preceq[u_i]$ by (1.1).
   253	
   254	Proper factors of $u_i$ are factors of $v_i$ or of $tv_i'$, hence lexmin; so $u_i\in\mathrm{MinRed}(A^+_{\{s,t\}})$.
   255	
   256	(b) All $m_{st}=2$ and $n\ge3$ (order $s_1<\dots<s_n$): $\Delta=s_1\cdots s_n$, the lexmin words are
   257	$s_1^{k_1}\cdots s_n^{k_n}$ and the normal ones are those missing a letter. $u_i=s_1s_2^{\,i}s_3\cdots s_n$
   258	($i\ge1$) is $\Delta$-divisible, and its maximal proper factors $s_1s_2^{\,i}s_3\cdots s_{n-1}$ and
   259	$s_2^{\,i}s_3\cdots s_n$ are normal. ∎
   260	
   261	(The words $u_i$ for $m=3$ are Bokut–Fong–Ke–Shiao's family $a_2a_1^la_2a_1$, arXiv:0806.1118, Lemma 4.1.)
   262	
   263	## 7. Type $B_3$: an explicit infinite GS basis — COMPUTED+VERIFIED (computer-assisted proof)
   264	
   265	Label the Coxeter graph $s_1 \overset{3}{—} s_2 \overset{4}{—} s_3$ and put
   266	$a=s_1,\ b=s_3,\ c=s_2$ with order $\Delta^{-1}<\Delta<a<b<c$ (bipartite order, middle node
   267	largest). So $m_{ac}=3$, $m_{bc}=4$, $m_{ab}=2$, $\Delta=(abc)^3$, $\varphi=\mathrm{id}$.
   268	
   269	**Theorem E.** The reduced GS basis of $A(B_3)$ on $\{\Delta^{-1},\Delta,a,b,c\}$ is
   270	
   271	| | leading word | right side | range |
   272	|---|---|---|---|
   273	| F1 | $ba$ | $ab$ | |
   274	| F2 | $cac$ | $aca$ | |
   275	| F3 | $c\,a^{i}\,aca$ | $acaa\,c^{i}$ | $i\ge1$ |
   276	| F4 | $cbcb$ | $bcbc$ | |
   277	| F5 | $c\,b^{i}\,bcbc$ | $bcbcc\,b^{i}$ | $i\ge1$ |
   278	| F6 | $cbc\,a^{i}\,b$ | $bcbc\,a^{i}$ | $i\ge1$ |
   279	| F7 | $ca\,b^{i}\,cbc$ | $acabc\,b^{i}$ | $i\ge1$ |
   280	| F8 | $c\,a^{i}\,bcabcb$ | $bcabcab\,c^{i}$ | $i\ge1$ |
   281	| F9 | $ca\,b^{i}\,c\,a^{j}\,bca$ | $acabca\,b^{i}c^{j}$ | $i,j\ge1$ |
   282	| F10 | $c\,a^{i}\,bcabc\,a^{j}\,b$ | $bcabcab\,c^{i}a^{j}$ | $i\ge2,\ j\ge1$ |
   283	| F11 | $cb\,b^{i}\,c\,a^{j}\,bca$ | $bcbcca\,b^{i}c^{j}$ | $i,j\ge1$ |
   284	| F12 | $ab\,b^{i}\,cabcabc$ | $\Delta\,b^{i}$ | $i\ge0$ |
   285	| T | $x\Delta,\ x\Delta^{-1}$ | $\Delta x,\ \Delta^{-1}x$ | $x\in\{a,b,c\}$ |
   286	| I | $\Delta\Delta^{-1},\ \Delta^{-1}\Delta$ | $1$ | |
   287	
   288	Normal forms: $\Delta^{(k)}w$ with $w\in\{a,b,c\}^*$ containing no leading word F1–F12.
   289	
   290	*Proof.* **Validity** (each relation holds in $A$ for all parameter values). Write a family as
   291	$A_0x_1^{p_1}A_1\cdots x_r^{p_r}A_r\to R\,y_1^{p_1}\cdots y_r^{p_r}$ (all families are of this
   292	shape). With $\rho_k=A_kA_{k+1}\cdots A_r$, the positive identities $x_k\rho_k=\rho_ky_k$ give
   293	$x_k^{p}\rho_k=\rho_ky_k^{p}$, hence moving the powers to the right from the last one,
   294	$\mathrm{LHS}=A_0A_1\cdots A_r\,y_1^{p_1}\cdots y_r^{p_r}$, and the base identity
   295	$A_0\cdots A_r=R$ finishes. The identities used, each with an explicit chain of braid moves in
   296	`evidence/certify/B3_derivations.txt` (e.g. $a\cdot bcabcb\to bacabcb\to bcacbcb\to bcabcbc$), are
   297	$$a\cdot aca=aca\cdot c,\ b\cdot bcbc=bcbc\cdot b,\ ab=ba,\ b\cdot cbc=cbc\cdot b,\ a\cdot bcabcb=bcabcb\cdot c,\
   298	b\cdot cbca=cbca\cdot b,\ a\cdot bca=bca\cdot c,\ b\cdot cabcabc=cabcabc\cdot b,$$
   299	$$caca=acaa,\ cbcbc=bcbcc,\ cbcb=bcbc,\ cacbc=acabc,\ cbcabcb=bcabcab,\ cacbca=acabca,\ cbcbca=bcbcca,\ abcabcabc=\Delta,$$
   300	and $x\Delta=\Delta x$ (G4). Each right side is deg-lex smaller (same length and smaller first
   301	letter, or shorter).
   302	
   303	**Completeness** (Lemma 1(iv)). The set of leading words is the regular language
   304	$\mathcal L=\{$F1–F12, T, I$\}$; the words avoiding $\mathcal L$ as factors are accepted by a
   305	deterministic automaton $\mathcal D$ with $K=57$ states (subset construction on the obvious
   306	NFA for $X^*\mathcal L$), so $I_S(t)=\sum_L|\mathrm{Irr}_L|t^L=P_1/Q_1$ with $\deg Q_1\le K$,
   307	$\deg P_1\le K-1$. By Lemma 2 and (G6), $G(t)=\frac{(1-t^9)(1+t)}{(1-t)D(t)}$ with
   308	$D(t)=1-3t+t^2+t^3+t^4-t^9$, so $G=P_2/Q_2$ with $\deg Q_2=10$, $\deg P_2\le 10$. The numerator
   309	of $I_S-G$ has degree $\le K+N+1=67$; the computed coefficients of $I_S$ and $G$ agree for
   310	$L=0,\dots,68$ (`work/certify.py B3`, `evidence/certify/B3.json`: `growth_certificate`), hence
   311	$I_S=G$, so $|\mathrm{Irr}_L|=|\mathrm{NF}_L|$ for all $L$, and by Lemma 1 the families form a GS
   312	basis for **all** lengths (this replaces the composition-by-composition check: by the CD lemma
   313	it is equivalent to the triviality of all compositions).
   314	
   315	**Reducedness.** No right side contains a leading word (checked for all parameter values by an
   316	automaton product, `rhs_irreducible`), so right sides are normal forms and the basis is the
   317	reduced one.
   318	
   319	**Independent second proof of completeness.** The positive leading words F1–F12 coincide, as
   320	regular languages, with the language of the Theorem B pair automaton for $B_3$ in this order
   321	(product exploration with 95 states, `language_equal_minred_automaton`). This route uses
   322	Lemma 4 instead of (G6). ∎
   323	
   324	**Second validity proof (group identities).** Independently of the braid-move chains,
   325	`check_validity` verifies, with the Garside normal form in the group, the identities that make
   326	each family valid for all parameter values: writing
   327	$\mathrm{LHS}=A_0x_1^{p_1}A_1\cdots x_r^{p_r}A_r$ and moving each power to the far right by conjugation,
   328	$\mathrm{LHS}=(A_0\cdots A_r)\,g_1^{p_1}\cdots g_r^{p_r}$ with $g_k=C_k^{-1}x_kC_k$, $C_k=A_k\cdots A_r$, and similarly
   329	for the right side; validity for all $p$ follows from $A_0\cdots A_r=B_0\cdots B_r$, $g_p=h_p$ for each
   330	parameter, and $g_pg_q=g_qg_p$ for parameters occurring in different orders on the two sides.
   331	
   332	Cross-checks (evidence only, bounded length): the oracle's reduced basis up to length 14 equals
   333	the 133 family instances of length $\le14$; the independent Knuth–Bendix completion
   334	(`work/kb.py`, which sees only the defining relations) returns exactly the same 133 rules of length
   335	$\le14$ when truncated at length 16 (`evidence/kb/kb_compare.jsonl`).
   336	
   337	**Theorem F (explicit bases for $D_4$, $H_3$, $A_3$).** For each (type, order) below the reduced
   338	GS basis of $A(W)$ on $\{\Delta^{\pm1}\}\cup S$ (deg-lex, $\Delta^{-1}<\Delta<S$) consists of the
   339	listed families — each of the form $u_0x_1^{k_1}u_1\cdots x_r^{k_r}u_r\to$ (explicit word with
   340	parameter-dependent runs), all $k_i\ge0$, $x_i$ letters — plus the twist rules
   341	$x\Delta^{\pm1}\to\Delta^{\pm1}\varphi(x)$ and the two cancellations. COMPUTED+VERIFIED (computer-assisted proof).
   342	
   343	| type, labelling | order | families | table | certificate (DFA states $K$; checked through $t^{K+N+2}$) |
   344	|---|---|---|---|---|
   345	| $D_4$: centre $s_2$, leaves $s_1,s_3,s_4$ | $s_1<s_3<s_4<s_2$ | 179 | `evidence/certify/D4_families.md` | $K=355$, through $t^{369}$; 616 product states |
   346	| $H_3$: $s_1\overset5—s_2—s_3$ | $s_3<s_1<s_2$ | 38 | `evidence/certify/H3_families.md` | $K=158$, through $t^{175}$ |
   347	| $H_3$ | $s_1<s_3<s_2$ | 58 | `evidence/certify/H3b_families.md` | $K=168$, through $t^{185}$ |
   348	| $A_3$: $s_1—s_2—s_3$ | $s_1<s_3<s_2$ | 14 | `evidence/certify/A3_families.md` | $K=32$, through $t^{40}$ |
   349	| $B_3$ (Theorem E, re-derived) | $s_1<s_3<s_2$ | 20 | `evidence/certify/B3auto_families.md` | $K=63$, through $t^{74}$ |
   350	
   351	For $D_4$ ($a=s_1,b=s_3,c=s_4,d=s_2$): $\Delta=(abcd)^3$ is central, $N=12$,
   352	$G(t)=\frac{(1-t^{12})(1+t)}{(1-t)D(t)}$ with $D(t)=1-4t+3t^2+2t^3-3t^6+t^{12}$ (16 parabolic subsets:
   353	3 commuting leaf pairs, 3 $A_2$ pairs, the $A_1^3$ of leaves, 3 $A_3$'s, $D_4$).
   354	
   355	*Proof.* The families were read off the Theorem B pair automaton: in these orders every cyclic
   356	strongly connected component of the trimmed automaton is a single state with one self-loop
   357	letter, so every accepting path spells $u_0x_1^{k_1}u_1\cdots$. Right sides were fitted as explicit
   358	words with parameter-dependent runs (`work/extract_families.py`). Each fitted list is then
   359	certified exactly as Theorem E (`work/certify.py D4|H3|H3b|A3|B3auto`, `evidence/certify/*.json`,
   360	all `ALL_OK`): validity for all parameters by the group identities above (every family; the
   361	positive braid-move transport applies to a subset and passes there); every right side deg-lex
   362	smaller; growth certificate (the table); language equality with the pair automaton; right sides
   363	irreducible (so the basis is reduced). Bounded cross-checks: for $D_4$ the oracle and the
   364	independent Knuth–Bendix completion both give the same 260 rules of length $\le10$; for the
   365	others the oracle agrees up to length 12. ∎
   366	
   367	**Corollary (classification of bounded bases).** For an irreducible finite Coxeter type $W$ the
   368	following are equivalent: (a) for some order of $S$ the leading words of the reduced deg-lex GS
   369	basis of $A(W)$ on the Artin–Garside alphabet form a finite union of families
   370	$u_0v_1^{*}u_1\cdots v_r^{*}u_r$ ($u_i,v_i$ words); (b) $\operatorname{rank}W\le3$ or $W=D_4$. Then the
   371	basis is finite iff $\operatorname{rank}W\le2$. *Proof.* (b)⇒(a): Theorems C, E, F. (a)⇒(b): such a
   372	union has polynomially bounded counts, while §8 gives exponential growth for every order when
   373	$W\in\{A_n,B_n\ (n\ge4),D_n\ (n\ge5),E_{6,7,8},F_4,H_4\}$. Status: COMPUTED+VERIFIED (relies on the
   374	computer-assisted parts of §§7–8).
   375	
   376	Remark. The order matters: for $B_3$ with the natural order $s_1<s_2<s_3$ the number of
   377	rules of length $L$ grows exponentially (§8), so no finite list of families of the above
   378	bounded shape exists for that order.
   379	
   380	## 8. Growth of the reduced basis: exact classification — COMPUTED+VERIFIED
   381	
   382	For a trim deterministic automaton $\mathcal A$ let $c(L)$ be the number of accepted words of
   383	length $L$. (i) If no SCC contains a cycle, the language is finite. (ii) If some SCC contains a
   384	vertex with two internal out-edges, there are two distinct cycles $C_1,C_2$ through it, and the
   385	$2^k$ concatenations of $C_1^{|C_2|}$ and $C_2^{|C_1|}$ give $2^k$ distinct accepted words of one
   386	length (determinism), so $\limsup_L c(L)^{1/L}>1$: exponential growth along an arithmetic
   387	progression of lengths (not necessarily at every length, e.g. $(aa|bb)^*$). (iii) Otherwise every
   388	cyclic SCC is a simple cycle; with $d\ge1$ the maximal number of cyclic SCCs on an accepting path,
   389	$c(L)=O(L^{d-1})$, and $c(L)\ge cL^{d-1}$ for $L$ in an arithmetic progression (independent
   390	repetition counts of $d$ cycles). We call (iii) "polynomial of degree $d-1$". Applying this to the
   391	exact pair automaton of Theorem B (`work/classify.py`, trimmed to co-accessible states) gives, for
   392	the positive part of the reduced GS basis of the group:
   393	
   394	| type | orders with polynomial growth (degree) | all other orders |
   395	|---|---|---|
   396	| $I_2(m)$ | both orders: finite (Theorem C) | — |
   397	| $A_3$ | middle node largest (2 orders): degree 1 | exponential |
   398	| $B_3$ | $s_1<s_3<s_2$: degree 1; $s_3<s_1<s_2$: degree 2 | exponential |
   399	| $H_3$ ($s_1\overset5—s_2—s_3$) | $s_1<s_3<s_2$: degree 3; $s_3<s_1<s_2$: degree 2 | exponential |
   400	| $D_4$ | centre largest (6 orders): degree 4 | exponential |
   401	| $A_4,B_4,F_4,H_4$ | none | exponential (all 24 orders) |
   402	
   403	For the reduced GS basis of the **monoid** $A^+(W)$ (option `--monoid`,
   404	`evidence/classify/monoid.jsonl`) the table is the same except for $I_2(m)$, $m\ge3$: there the
   405	monoid basis is infinite of polynomial degree 0 (Theorem D(a); Autord, arXiv:0712.0525), while the
   406	group basis is finite; for $m=2$ both are finite.
   407	
   408	By Lemma 3, $P(W)\supseteq\mathrm{MinRed}(A^+_J)$ for every proper parabolic $J$; the monoid
   409	classification for $J=A_4,B_4$ therefore shows: for $A_n$ ($n\ge5$), $B_n$ ($n\ge5$), $D_n$
   410	($n\ge5$, which contain $A_4$) and $E_6,E_7,E_8$ (which contain $A_4$), **every** order gives
   411	exponential growth (in the sense of (ii)) of the number of rules of length $L$. Consequently, for
   412	these types and for $A_4,B_4,F_4,H_4$, no GS basis for any deg-lex order of our form has a set of
   413	leading words that is a finite union of families $u_0x_1^{p_1}u_1\cdots x_r^{p_r}u_r$ (such a union
   414	has polynomially bounded counts, and every GS basis contains all minimal non-normal words as
   415	leading words, Lemma 1(c)). This is a computer-assisted statement: it depends on Lemma 4/Theorem B,
   416	on the validated tables (§5), and on `work/automaton.py`/`work/classify.py`, whose output was
   417	cross-checked against the independent enumeration and the growth series.
   418	
   419	## 9. What is not solved here
   420	
   421	* Explicit bases on the Artin–Garside alphabet for $A_n,B_n$ ($n\ge4$), $D_n$ ($n\ge5$),
   422	  $E_6,E_7,E_8$, $F_4$, $H_4$ (in any order), and for the rank-3 types and $D_4$ in the orders with
   423	  exponential growth. For each of them Theorem B gives the exact basis as a finite automaton
   424	  (computable from $W$), but no closed form is given here; by §8 any closed form must use
   425	  families that are not of bounded type (e.g. Bokut-style free sub-words). For $B_4$ the trimmed
   426	  automaton has only two strongly connected components with more than one cycle (6 states each,
   427	  in the order $s_1<s_3<s_4<s_2$), which suggests the next target.
   428	* Other monomial orders (weighted, tower, non-deg-lex): not studied; whether some order gives a
   429	  bounded or finite description for rank $\ge3$ is open here.
   430	* Reducible types are covered by Theorems A–D but were not run through §§7–8.
   431	
   432	## 10. Check of the published type-$B_3$ monoid basis (Iqbal–Munir–Ayub–Nizami 2024) — COMPUTED+VERIFIED
   433	
   434	Iqbal, Munir, Ayub, Nizami, *Complete presentation and Hilbert series of the mixed braid monoid
   435	$MB_{1,3}$*, Rev. Un. Mat. Argentina 67(2) (2024) 503–516, Theorem 2.3, give a complete
   436	presentation (GS basis) of $MB_{1,3}$, which is the Artin **monoid** of type $B_3$
   437	($z_1\overset4—z_2—z_3$), in the standard generators, length-lex with $z_1<z_2<z_3$ (no
   438	$\Delta^{\pm1}$; a monoid, not the group). Checked here (`work/check_iqbal2024.py`,
   439	`work/certify_iqbal2024.py`):
   440	* As printed, $R_3^{(4)}$, $R_6^{(4)}$, $R_9^{(4)}$ are length-inconsistent ($|{\rm LHS}|=|{\rm RHS}|-1$ for
   441	  every parameter value), so no instance is a valid relation. With the evident correction
   442	  $z_2^{\,n}\mapsto z_2^{\,n+1}$ on their left sides (as in $R_1^{(4)}$), every relation holds for all
   443	  parameters (transport identities plus $x\,z_3z_2z_1z_2z_3=z_3z_2z_1z_2z_3\,x$, $x\in\{z_1,z_2\}$, for the free tails).
   444	* The corrected list is a GS basis for all lengths: the words avoiding its leading words are
   445	  counted by a 65-state automaton whose counts agree with $M(t)=1/D(t)$ up to length 76
   446	  (rational-function argument of §7), and every minimal non-lexmin word (Theorem B automaton,
   447	  monoid version) is one of its leading words.
   448	
   449	So the published basis is correct up to three misprints. It concerns the monoid in the natural
   450	order; it does not give the group basis on the Artin–Garside alphabet (Theorem A shows how the
   451	group basis differs: $\Delta$-divisible minimal words must be added and words with
   452	$\Delta$-divisible proper factors removed).

